# Open readings — 2026-11-16
#
# Three calls from the babble journal's open facet. Each has readings and the output that bears on them. Nothing here is decided, and no reader was changed.

#>journal_2026_11_16_open_readings
#:cache #!observation
#:layer #!pragmatics

^["entry"]{
 date: "2026-11-16"
 kind: #observation
 base: "this repo at 4aa1b92b, after keyed pivots landed opt-in and left the colon-equals turn and the particle turn unoffered"
 method: "readLine, spokenLine, lex and parse on the lines named below; pivotsOf with the keyed option off and on; runSpw on five wonder forms; the wonder case read beside those values; one swap of the key colon for an equals over the same 500 lines the keyed-pivot test hashes"
 limits: "three calls of the open facet, not the rest; the swap was counted and not offered; a warning's timestamp was not compared"
}

^["key_relation"]{
 question: "Is a turn between the key colon and an equals a pivot?"
 held: "With the keyed option off, pivotsOf of %root: 293.66 offers root:% 293.66, !root: 293.66 and $root: 293.66, and the line reader reads none of them back: the word is %root: and the rest is a space then 293.66."
 offered: "With the keyed option on, the same line offers root%: 293.66, which the line reader reads and the parser and the spoken reader do not, and !root: 293.66 and $root: 293.66, which all three read. Every offered line still contains the colon."
 swap_one: "Replacing that colon with an equals gives %root= 293.66. The word is %root= and the rest is still a space then 293.66. Spoken says measure root=, 293.66 with unread 0. Parse reports 0 errors and 0 warnings. The line reader does not read it whole, because the rest is not empty and the line is no longer a key."
 spaced: "key = value, with spaces around the equals, has word key and rest a space, an equals, a space, and value, and spoken says key, configuration value. key= value, glued, has word key= and rest a space then value, and spoken says key=, value. Both parse with 0 errors and 0 warnings."
 corpus: "Of the 500 lines, 162 are keyed. Replacing the key colon with an equals leaves a non-empty rest on 155, so the line reader would not read those swaps whole. 154 parse with no error or warning, and 135 have spoken unread 0."
 absorbed: "The 7 whose rest becomes empty all have a mode as the value. improve_theory: [@development, @theory_review] becomes improve_theory= [@development, @theory_review], spoken says improve theory=, mode, list of 2, and parse is clean with unread 0. The spoken line no longer calls it a key."
 reading_keep: "The colon is the key's end and not a mark, so it does not turn. The mark on the name turns, and the value stays the rest. This is what the keyed option already does, and the release line key = value stays a word plus a rest."
 reading_swap: "The colon and the equals are one relation written two ways, so a keyed line would offer the equals in the colon's place. Against that, the swap keeps a rest on 155 of 162 lines, the spoken reader stops calling the line a key, and a glued equals is said as part of the word."
 reading_space: "Only the spaced equals is the configuration mark, and a glued equals is a character of the word. Against that, both spellings parse clean on the lines above, so the parser does not enforce the space."
 first: "Keep the count, and do not offer the swap: the same 500 lines, the key colon replaced by an equals, rest and parse and spoken recorded. An offer later would have to say which of the 7 absorbed modes it means to read back."
}

^["particle_turn"]{
 question: "May a turn change one character of a particle, ~#goal: into ~.goal: or ~@goal:, when a particle is read from its parts?"
 held: "lex of ~#goal: is one annotation spanning ~#goal and one colon. pivotsOf of ~#goal: one with the keyed option on offers one line, goal#~: one, marked not read. It does not offer ~.goal: one or ~@goal: one."
 side: "goal#~: one has an empty word and rest a space then one. Spoken says goal#, potential, unread :, one, with unread 1. Parse has 0 errors and 0 warnings. The line reader and the spoken reader both miss it."
 dot: "~.goal: one lexes as operator ~, operator ., identifier goal, colon. The word is ~.goal: and the rest is a space then one. Spoken says potential ground key goal, one, with unread 0. Parse has 0 errors and 1 warning: structured parse stopped at the operator dot and the surface degraded to prose."
 at: "~@goal: one lexes as operator ~, operator @, identifier goal, colon. The word is empty and the rest is a space then one, because @ is read as a bond. Spoken says potential perspective, key goal, one, with unread 0. Parse has 0 errors and 0 warnings."
 reading_token: "A particle is one token. A turn may move the whole prefix and may not rewrite one character inside the token. Against that, the side move is the one pivot on offer, and neither the line reader nor the spoken reader reads it back."
 reading_parts: "The particle is two marks and a name, so the second mark turns to its neighbours, dot and @. Against that, the dot form degrades to prose and the @ form splits into a bond, so neither is a line the three readers read back together."
 reading_side_only: "A side move of a particle is in bounds and an interior turn is not. This is what the keyed option does. Against that, the allowed move is the one marked not read."
 first: "Lock the three lines as a fixture before any offer: ~#goal: one offers only the side move, ~.goal: one carries the prose warning, and ~@goal: one has an empty word. The fixture records output and does not add the turns."
}

^["guard"]{
 question: "Is ?[false]{ yes } giving true a defect, or is a wonder with a mode not a guard at all?"
 values: "runSpw returns success and the value true for ?[false]{ yes }, ?[true]{ yes }, ?{ yes } and ?[0]{ yes }. ?{ } returns success and the value null."
 shape: "The mode is a string on the probe frame, false or true or 0, and it is not an argument. The only argument is the body, and that body's id is yes. The empty body has no id."
 case: "The wonder case reads the first argument as the condition and returns the second, or true when the second is missing. It does not read the probe frame. A truthy body and a missing second argument return the boolean true, not the word yes."
 reading_defect: "The mode was the condition and the case left it unread, so the strings false, true and 0 all collapse to the same boolean. Against that, the mode is stored as a string name, which is a strange shape for a boolean condition."
 reading_not_guard: "A wonder with a mode is a named probe, and the mode is not required to change the value. Against that, the value is the boolean true rather than the body yes, and the empty body is the only one of the five that returns null."
 reading_body: "The body is the condition, and a truthy body with no alternative returns true, the mode sitting beside it as a label. Against that, nothing in the line tells a label from a condition, and the value cannot tell ?[false]{ yes } from ?[true]{ yes }."
 first: "A fixture of the five forms and their values, and of the fact that the wonder case does not read the probe frame. That locks today. A guard, if one is wanted later, has to say what the string false means before it can return null."
}
